UNIT word_diff
LANG ts
PRIMARY-SHA256 f45b61112159279c9411e046d62f3489959604337b9c74a1b3ca1f321da41a9c
SIG export function wordDiff(a: string, b: string): { op: '=' | '-' | '+'; text: string }[]
ABI TypeScript compiles to the js runtime and is driven exactly as js: wordDiff(a, b) positionally. text = string (UTF-16
  code units). The result is a fresh array of plain objects { op, text }. STRICT: the artifact type-checks under strict: true
  with no any, no non-null assertions (!), no @ts-ignore and no @ts-expect-error; reading an array element past a bound is
  undefined at runtime, so index reads are guarded or typed as possibly undefined, never asserted. A throw (R9) is a thrown
  Error; the message is not graded. ES module with the named export wordDiff; imports nothing.
ENVELOPE as js: a non-string a or b reaches the artifact at runtime despite the typed SIG (js-only probe) and must throw, as
  the package does when it calls .split on it; never coerce it with String().
MECHANISM (restated from js — language files are self-contained) the token cut (R2) is String.prototype.split with the
  TOKENS pin, which keeps the separators and yields R2's list with its leading and trailing empty strings. JS \s is exactly
  the WS pin. WORDS (R5) may be text.trim().split(/\s+/).length on a text with a word character; test a whitespace-only text with the
  WS-ONLY pin first. The result drops entries with empty text (R8). Token equality is ===.
  The LCS table may be an array of Uint32Array rows.
DEPENDS-ON none
PINNED regex TOKENS = /(\s+)/ CITED R2 — the capture group keeps the whitespace runs as tokens
PINNED regex WS-ONLY = /^\s*$/ CITED R6 — a whitespace-only or empty glue candidate, 0 words
PROVENANCE rederive@c41c5fcd84e0 cli/lsp/explain.mjs wordDiff. Language file cut 2026-11-02 with the primary.
